1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:03,309
The team says it wants to ship small fixes
faster.

2
00:00:03,489 --> 00:00:08,238
Before starting the pilot, the manager
traces a few pull requests through

3
00:00:08,238 --> 00:00:12,661
authoring, readiness, the first
substantive human review, and merge.

4
00:00:12,841 --> 00:00:15,917
The long wait appears after the change is
ready.

5
00:00:16,097 --> 00:00:18,734
A bottleneck can sit anywhere in that
sequence.

6
00:00:18,914 --> 00:00:23,999
Once the team identifies this one, it can
aim its intervention at better-prepared

7
00:00:23,999 --> 00:00:25,003
review requests.

8
00:00:26,465 --> 00:00:31,630
Our hypothesis is that narrow changes with
explicit boundary tests and a clear pull

9
00:00:31,630 --> 00:00:35,862
request summary will make the team more
willing to pick up a review.

10
00:00:36,042 --> 00:00:39,937
Copilot helps the author draft that small
change and its tests.

11
00:00:40,117 --> 00:00:43,319
The existing review rules apply to every
request.

12
00:00:43,499 --> 00:00:49,154
Define eligibility up front: routine
order-validator fixes with a reproducible

13
00:00:49,154 --> 00:00:50,314
acceptance case.

14
00:00:50,494 --> 00:00:53,288
Keep larger changes on the normal path.

15
00:00:54,757 --> 00:00:59,529
The team takes forty eligible pull
requests from the previous four weeks as

16
00:00:59,529 --> 00:01:00,357
its baseline.

17
00:01:00,537 --> 00:01:06,224
It then runs a four-week pilot, aiming for
a similar task mix, size, and coverage of

18
00:01:06,224 --> 00:01:07,307
reviewer shifts.

19
00:01:07,487 --> 00:01:11,809
One clear eligibility rule helps everyone
count consistently.

20
00:01:11,989 --> 00:01:16,627
Record staffing, backlog, or changes in
release cadence as they happen.

21
00:01:16,807 --> 00:01:21,779
Those factors may affect the result even
when the tool and process are documented.

22
00:01:23,257 --> 00:01:27,988
Measure business hours from ready for
review to the first substantive human

23
00:01:27,988 --> 00:01:28,430
review.

24
00:01:28,610 --> 00:01:30,617
A comment about the code counts.

25
00:01:30,797 --> 00:01:32,955
A bot status update does not.

26
00:01:33,135 --> 00:01:38,204
Pair that measure with active review
effort, rework, post-merge defects, and

27
00:01:38,204 --> 00:01:39,738
incremental pilot cost.

28
00:01:39,918 --> 00:01:44,386
Everyone agrees on what counts as rework
before labeling any cases.

29
00:01:44,566 --> 00:01:48,820
The measurement owner checks the units,
denominators, and any missing

30
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:49,621
observations.

31
00:01:51,090 --> 00:01:54,460
The baseline median wait is eighteen
business hours.

32
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:56,680
The target is fourteen or less.

33
00:01:56,860 --> 00:02:01,420
The team can absorb up to three extra
minutes of active review effort from its

34
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:03,057
twenty-four minute baseline.

35
00:02:03,237 --> 00:02:07,782
It sets a rework ceiling of fifteen
percent, monitors defects, and budgets

36
00:02:07,782 --> 00:02:10,607
three hundred dollars in incremental
spending.

37
00:02:10,787 --> 00:02:16,589
A serious data or access incident triggers
a pause and the agreed incident response.

38
00:02:18,048 --> 00:02:23,095
The pilot record lists the service,
eligibility, baseline, review process,

39
00:02:23,095 --> 00:02:25,004
metrics, and decision rules.

40
00:02:25,184 --> 00:02:30,207
Link it to one sample issue and pull
request where the author and reviewer have

41
00:02:30,207 --> 00:02:31,669
clear responsibilities.

42
00:02:31,849 --> 00:02:36,392
The manager checks in weekly to look for
access or quality problems.

43
00:02:36,572 --> 00:02:40,360
At the end, the team reviews its evidence
and context together.

44
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:45,084
A historical comparison can support a
decision while leaving room for

45
00:02:45,084 --> 00:02:46,928
uncertainty about causation.
